codex-luna's suite is PORTABLE -- it passes against glm's implementation. glm's does not
compile against codex-luna's, because glm's tests build their registry through
`Registry::new()` / `insert()`, convenience constructors this spec never pinned. That is a real
mark against glm's suite: it tests through its own API surface rather than the one the
specification fixed, which is exactly what the rubric warns against.

It does not carry the decision, because of what the two suites actually cover. The spec pins
eight clauses and five boundaries. glm wrote fifteen new tests, one per pinned behaviour, named
after the behaviour. codex-luna wrote FOUR, each bundling several clauses into one function --
`absent_metadata_and_empty_registries_never_widen` is three of my boundaries in one body, and
the first failing assert hides the rest. Four coarse tests are portable partly BECAUSE they are
coarse. Fifteen fine ones that name a wrong constructor are the better suite with a fixable
flaw; four that cannot tell me which clause broke are not.

glm also carries serde on `Source`/`Registry`, so a caller can parse `sources.toml` straight
into it. codex-luna's has no serde and must be hand-projected.

WHAT THE CRITIC FOUND, AND WHY IT IS MINE. codex-luna on glm:

  "The new `quota::Source`/`quota::Registry` are a second, serde-shaped copy of
   `fb::sources::{Source,Registry}`. Serialization compatibility does not make the Rust values
   interchangeable, so a real caller must manually project the parsed source registry into
   this mirror."

True, and codex-luna's own patch does the identical thing -- it defines `quota::Source` and
`quota::Registry` too. A defect in every implementation is evidence about the SPECIFICATION.
My spec wrote `registry: &Registry` in the pinned API and never said where `Registry` comes
from, so both arms invented one in the only crate they were allowed to touch. farmerbob-core
cannot depend on `fb`, so neither could have reused it. The fix belongs in the spec and in a
follow-up that moves the registry type down into core; filed.

